// One word that more than one core reads or writes without a lock, as a type
// (GitHub issue #669, #637 stage 1 group B).
//
// The five atomic intrinsics take a bare `usize` address, so each user used to
// write a helper that turned a global or an array element into one and then an
// `unsafe` block around the intrinsic. Nothing said that the address was a
// word meant to be accessed atomically, or that an index was in range. An
// `AtomicWord` is one `usize` of storage (the same layout, so zero-initialised
// storage and an external debugger's view of it are unchanged), and the
// compiler's built-in operations atomic_word_load / _store / _swap /
// _fetch_add / _compare_exchange take a pointer to one (GitHub issue #637
// stage 3, step C). They need no `unsafe`: the field is private to this
// file, so the cell is reached only through them, and each is one atomic
// instruction. A per-CPU word is an array of them
// indexed by a refined core number, which removes the address arithmetic along
// with its `unsafe` block. The ordering stays in each operation's name, as it
// is in the intrinsics (SPEC.md, "Atomic Operations").
//
// The compiler, not this file, turns a cell into the intrinsics' address.
// The allowlist in scripts/check_lock_discipline.py lists every file that
// still calls an intrinsic directly, with its reason.
//
// Current limitations (2026-10-01). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - IT DOES NOT CHECK THE ORDERING ARGUMENT. Whether a release store here
//   pairs with an acquire load there is a property of two accesses, which no
//   type of one word can see (GitHub issue #613 asks the type system to carry
//   it). What this fixes is WHICH word is accessed.
// - A CELL CAN STILL BE READ WITH A PLAIN LOAD. `value` is private to this
//   file, so a user cannot name it, but the storage is an ordinary global and
//   a raw pointer cast from its address reads it unsynchronised.
// - The lock word is a cell too: Mutex and TaskMutex hold an AtomicWord and
//   kernel/lib/spinlock.tkb works on it through the same operations.

struct no_copy AtomicWord { private value: usize; }
